Here's a recommended structure:

**1. Make HTTP calls injectable**

```csharp
// In your MCP server project
public class MyTool
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;
    public MyTool(HttpClient httpClient) => _httpClient = httpClient;

    public async Task<string> GetDataAsync(string id)
    {
        var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync($"https://api.example.com/data/{id}");
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
    }
}
```

**2. Create the test project**

```bash
dotnet new xunit -n MyMcpServer.Tests
cd MyMcpServer.Tests
dotnet add reference ../MyMcpServer/MyMcpServer.csproj
```

**3. Use `MockHttpMessageHandler` to fake API responses**

```csharp
// TestHelpers/MockHttpMessageHandler.cs
public class MockHttpMessageHandler : HttpMessageHandler
{
    private readonly Func<HttpRequestMessage, Task<HttpResponseMessage>> _handler;

    public MockHttpMessageHandler(Func<HttpRequestMessage, Task<HttpResponseMessage>> handler)
        => _handler = handler;

    protected override Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(
        HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken ct)
        => _handler(request);
}
```

**4. Write tests**

```csharp
public class MyToolTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task GetDataAsync_ReturnsExpectedData()
    {
        var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler(request =>
        {
            Assert.Equal("/data/123", request.RequestUri!.AbsolutePath);
            return Task.FromResult(new HttpResponseMessage(HttpStatusCode.OK)
            {
                Content = new StringContent("""{"name":"test"}""")
            });
        });

        var tool = new MyTool(new HttpClient(handler)
        {
            BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com")
        });

        var result = await tool.GetDataAsync("123");
        Assert.Contains("test", result);
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task GetDataAsync_ThrowsOnServerError()
    {
        var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler(_ =>
            Task.FromResult(new HttpResponseMessage(HttpStatusCode.InternalServerError)));

        var tool = new MyTool(new HttpClient(handler)
        {
            BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com")
        });

        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<HttpRequestException>(
            () => tool.GetDataAsync("123"));
    }
}
```

**Key principles:**

- **Inject `HttpClient`** (or `IHttpClientFactory`) into tools — never `new HttpClient()` inline
- **`MockHttpMessageHandler`** lets you fake responses without any third-party mocking library (or use [Moq](https://github.com/moq/moq4) / [NSubstitute](https://nsubstitute.github.io/) if you prefer)
- **Test each tool method** with success, error, and edge-case responses
- **Project layout:**
  ```
  src/MyMcpServer/          ← MCP server + tools
  tests/MyMcpServer.Tests/  ← xUnit test project
      TestHelpers/           ← MockHttpMessageHandler, fixtures
      MyToolTests.cs
  ```

This keeps your tools testable and your tests fast since no real HTTP calls are made.
